[{"id":122729,"sender_id":494,"sender_full_name":"PaoloPerrone","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ebe8d69259f6a6c8fe452266ed4c80a6?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Functor.20which.20is.20a.20.27coboundary.27/near/122729\">#1</a> Sometimes I’ve heard people calling ‘coboundary’ a functor which identifies any two parallel arrows. That is, it makes every diagram commute, or ‘it only depends on the endpoints’.</p>\n<p>Have these functors been studied? (Outside the special case of, say, differential forms?)</p>\n<p>I’m mostly interested in higher versions, e,g, a 2-functor whose action on the 2-cells only depends on the domain and codomain 1-cells.</p>","timestamp":1762330901,"subject":"Functor which is a 'coboundary'","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":122730,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Functor.20which.20is.20a.20.27coboundary.27/near/122730\">#2</a> In the realm of simplicial sets such maps are those that factor through an <em><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span>-<a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/coskeleton\">coskeleton</a></em> (the result of filling all boundaries of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m \\gt n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5782em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">m</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&gt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span>-simplices and higher).</p>\n<p>(It’s these <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">m</span></span></span></span>-simplices that I would call “coboundaries”, not the functor that quotients them out.)</p>","timestamp":1762335698,"subject":"Functor which is a 'coboundary'","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]}]